# 10. Primary Improvement Metrics

The system must compare baseline and post-learning results.

## 10.1 Correctness

Measure:

```text
baseline_correctness
post_learning_correctness
improvement
```

Example:

```text
Baseline:       70%
After learning: 90%

Improvement:    +20 percentage points
```

---

# 11. Evaluation Quality

Measure:

```text
average_quality
average_confidence
```

Example:

```text
Baseline quality:       2.8 / 4
After learning:         3.5 / 4
```

The exact scoring mechanism should reuse the existing Phase 3 evaluation model.

---

# 12. Strategy Improvement

Record:

```text
selected_strategy
historical_success_rate
```

Determine whether the system begins selecting strategies that historically perform better.

Example:

```text
Initial:

direct_calculation
success rate = 65%

verify_calculation
success rate = 90%
```

After sufficient experience:

```text
Selected strategy:
verify_calculation
```

This demonstrates strategy adaptation.

---

# 13. Workflow Improvement

Record:

```text
selected_workflow
workflow_success_rate
```

Example:

```text
Initial workflow:

Orchestrator
 → Solver
 → Evaluator

Later:

Orchestrator
 → Planner
 → Retrieval
 → Strategy
 → Solver
 → Critic
 → Improvement
 → Final Answer
```

The system should choose the more effective workflow when historical evidence supports it.

---

# 14. Agent Improvement

Record:

```text
agent_id
execution_count
success_count
failure_count
success_rate
average_quality
average_confidence
average_duration
```

The system must demonstrate that historical agent performance can influence future agent selection.

---

# 15. Retrieval Improvement

Measure:

```text
retrieved_experience_count
retrieved_lesson_count
retrieval_similarity
retrieval_relevance
```

The validation should confirm that learned lessons relevant to the current task are retrieved.

However:

> Retrieval alone must NOT be considered proof of improvement.

The final task result must also be evaluated.

---

# 16. Retry and Improvement Behavior

Record:

```text
retry_count
critic_triggered
improvement_triggered
final_answer_after_improvement
```

Example:

```text
Initial answer
     ↓
Critic detects problem
     ↓
Improvement agent
     ↓
Revised answer
     ↓
Final evaluation
```

The system should demonstrate that the agent can improve an answer within the same task.

---

# 17. Latency

Measure:

```text
baseline_duration_ms
post_learning_duration_ms
```

Learning should not automatically make the system significantly slower.

If quality improves while latency increases, the report should clearly show the trade-off.

---

# 18. Improvement Score

Create a benchmark summary containing at least:

```text
Correctness
Quality
Confidence
Strategy effectiveness
Workflow effectiveness
Agent effectiveness
Retry rate
Improvement rate
Latency
```

The system may calculate an overall improvement score, but the individual metrics must always remain visible.

Do NOT hide all results behind a single score.

---

# 19. Continuous Improvement Must Not Mean Monotonic Improvement

The system must NOT assume:

```text
Run 1 = 70%
Run 2 = 75%
Run 3 = 80%
Run 4 = 85%
```

Real systems may fluctuate.

Instead, demonstrate improvement statistically or over multiple benchmark runs.

Example:

```text
Run 1: 70%
Run 2: 75%
Run 3: 73%
Run 4: 82%
Run 5: 85%
```

This can still represent improvement.

---

# 20. Multiple Runs

Because Llama 3.2 is probabilistic, a single run must not be treated as definitive proof.

Support:

```text
BENCHMARK_RUNS=3
```

or higher.

For example:

```text
Baseline

Run 1
Run 2
Run 3

Post-learning

Run 1
Run 2
Run 3
```

Calculate aggregate results.

---

# 21. Benchmark Isolation

Benchmark data must be identifiable.

Add:

```text
benchmark_run_id
benchmark_phase
benchmark_mode
```

Possible modes:

```text
baseline
learning
evaluation
```

This prevents benchmark records from becoming mixed with ordinary application experiences.

